# Cubed sphere, A SHOCK THROUGH A SEAM AND A CUBE VERTEX.  Build with -D PROBLEM=cs_test.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS.  Every other cubed-sphere test in this directory is a SMOOTH field, and
# several are exact equilibria, so none of them says anything about shock capturing.  Two
# pieces of the seam machinery are built on smoothness and are worth testing on a jump:
# the along-seam resample is an UNLIMITED 3-point Lagrange quadratic, and the cube-vertex
# corner is an UNLIMITED extrapolation amplifying its input by 7 (d=1) to 17 (d=2).  Both
# are exactly the shapes that overshoot on a discontinuity, and `negative internal energy
# at panel corners` is already a known symptom on SMOOTH problems.
#
# THE GATE NEEDS NO EXACT SOLUTION.  On a sphere the blast problem is invariant under
# rotation, so the answer is spherically symmetric about the blast centre wherever that
# centre sits relative to the grid.  So:
#
#   WITHIN a run   bin every cell by geodesic angle from the centre; the spread inside a
#                  bin is grid imprinting, and is zero for a perfect scheme.
#   BETWEEN runs   the SAME blast centred in a panel INTERIOR, on a SEAM, and on a cube
#                  VERTEX must agree.  The interior run is the control; the others differ
#                  from it ONLY in where the grid sits under identical physics.
#
# THREE PLACEMENTS, by overriding problem/blast_x,y,z (they are normalised internally):
#
#   INTERIOR (control)   blast_x=0   blast_y=0   blast_z=1     panel centre
#   SEAM                 blast_x=1   blast_y=0   blast_z=1     edge midpoint, 2 panels
#   VERTEX               blast_x=1   blast_y=1   blast_z=1     cube vertex, 3 panels
#
# The overpressure is uniform in RADIUS, so the shock runs TANGENTIALLY -- which is what
# drives it through the seam.  Radial boundaries are reflecting to keep it that way rather
# than draining the blast radially.
#
# WHAT WAS MEASURED (nx2 = 32, tlim = 0.30, blast_ang = 0.25, varying blast_p, i.e. the
# initial pressure CONTRAST against p0 = 1):
#
#   centred in a panel INTERIOR   survives 100,  fails at 300
#   centred on a SEAM             survives  50,  fails at  70
#   centred on a cube VERTEX      survives  50,  fails at  70
#
# THREE CONCLUSIONS, and the first is the one that matters most:
#
#  1. A SHOCK CROSSES A SEAM PERFECTLY WELL.  The interior blast at contrast 100 drives
#     its shock out past the seam (front at 1.163 rad against a seam at 0.785, with p ~ 28
#     still at the seam) and finishes clean.  Seams are NOT a barrier to a passing shock.
#  2. What the seam costs is HEADROOM: with the strong region sitting ON a seam the
#     tolerated contrast drops by roughly 2-4x.  A cube VERTEX is no worse than a seam --
#     both fail between 50 and 70 -- which is worth knowing, because the vertex corner is
#     filled by an UNLIMITED extrapolation and might have been much worse.
#  3. The interior fails too, at 300.  So this is not "seams are broken"; it is a
#     robustness limit of the scheme everywhere, lowered near a seam.  The usual remedy,
#     first-order flux correction, is a startup FATAL on the cubed sphere
#     ({hydro,mhd}_fofc.cpp have no gnomonic form), so there is no fallback to switch on.
#
# THE FAILURE IS SILENT.  Nothing aborts and no floor is reported: the run finishes
# normally, prints "Terminating on time limit", and every cell is NaN.  That is why this
# check counts NON-FINITE cells explicitly -- it is the only thing that notices.
#
# WHAT TO READ.  CSTestBlastCheck prints, in order: the spherical-symmetry residual over
# the whole domain and restricted to cells within 2 cells of a panel edge; the max
# pressure overall and near a seam, with how far the front has run; min pressure and
# density; and the NON-FINITE count.
#
# HONEST LIMIT OF THE SYMMETRY RESIDUAL: as defined it is (max-min)/mean inside a geodesic
# bin, so a bin straddling the shock front reports the FRONT'S OWN WIDTH, not imprinting,
# and is O(1) even for a perfect scheme.  Use it to compare the SAME time between the
# three placements, not as an absolute.  The primary gates here are the NON-FINITE count
# and the strength threshold above.
#
# AXES: x1 is RADIAL, x2 = xi and x3 = eta are the panel-tangential angles.  Must be 3D.

<comment>
problem = cubed sphere blast through a panel seam and a cube vertex

<job>
basename = cs_blast

<mesh>
nghost   = 2
nx1      = 8            # RADIAL direction
x1min    = 1.0
x1max    = 2.0
ix1_bc   = reflect
ox1_bc   = reflect

nx2      = 32           # xi;  nx2 must equal nx3 (Mesh checks this)
x2min    = -1.0
x2max    = 1.0
ix2_bc   = panel
ox2_bc   = panel

nx3      = 32           # eta
x3min    = -1.0
x3max    = 1.0
ix3_bc   = panel
ox3_bc   = panel

use_cubed_sphere = true

<meshblock>
nx1      = 8
nx2      = 32
nx3      = 32

<time>
evolution  = dynamic
integrator = rk2
cfl_number = 0.3
nlim       = -1
tlim       = 0.30
ndiag      = 1000

<hydro>
eos         = ideal
reconstruct = plm
rsolver     = hllc
gamma       = 1.666667

<problem>
iprob      = 12
d0         = 1.0
p0         = 1.0
blast_p    = 100.0      # strong: a Mach ~4 shock, not a linear wave
blast_ang  = 0.25       # angular radius of the overpressure cap, radians
blast_x    = 1.0        # centre direction; DEFAULT here is the CUBE VERTEX
blast_y    = 1.0
blast_z    = 1.0
